# AgentGuard-FL — revision code

Code for *"AgentGuard-FL: A Robust Agentic Federated Intrusion Detection System Against
Confidence-Aware Label-Flipping Attacks"*, updated for the reviewer comments.
Equation / algorithm numbers in comments refer to the paper.

### Where each reviewer comment is handled
| Comment | What the code does | Output |
|---|---|---|
| R1-1 metrics inconsistent | Every metric is computed from one confusion matrix (TP, TN, FP, FN) on one fixed test set; a check recomputes them; test-set class counts are saved | `table_*.csv`, `per_run_final.csv`, `test_class_distribution.csv`, `cm_*.png` |
| R1-2, R2-4, R3-2 robust baselines | Krum and Trimmed Mean on the same model | `table_main.csv` |
| R2-3, R3-5, R4-1 other attacks | ALIE ("A Little Is Enough") model poisoning, which leaves labels clean | `table_main.csv` |
| R1-2, R2-6, R3-1 ablation | Each client component and each trust term switched off in turn | `table_ablation.csv` |
| R1-3, R2 (Q1), R3-4 partitioning | Per-client class counts, heterogeneity score, Dirichlet α sweep | `client_distribution_*.csv`, `table_heterogeneity.csv` |
| R1-3, R2-2, R3-3 single seed | `SEEDS` list, mean ± std, 95% CI | all tables |
| R2-1/5, R2 (Q3) trusted telemetry | Attackers never run the agent; optional fully forged telemetry | `table_forged_telemetry.csv` |
| R2 (Q4) hyperparameter sensitivity | Sweeps over κ_q, λ_R, γ_q, τ | `table_sensitivity.csv` |
| R1 minor, R2 (Q6), R3-6 why Tiny-BERT | Same pipeline with a random frozen BERT, a BERT trained from scratch, and an MLP | `table_backbone.csv` |
| R4-2 "Baseline-FedAvg" | Renamed **FedAvg** = standard FedAvg [McMahan et al. 2017] on the same model, no defence | — |
| R4-3 Table III metrics | FedRNN / FedCNN / FedLSTM with all metrics under CA-LFA, RLFA and ALIE | `table_models.csv` |

**Threat model — the same for every defence.** Malicious clients control their own data and
training. Label-flipping attackers poison their labels (training and private validation part)
and run Algorithm 1; the ALIE attacker trains honestly and then submits a crafted update. They
never run the AgentGuard agent. By default they report telemetry computed honestly from their own
data; with `attacker_forges_telemetry=True` they report perfect telemetry instead. The update
norm used by the server is measured by the server from the received update, so it cannot be faked.

**Long runs.** Results are checkpointed to `results/all_rounds.csv` after every run; re-running
the notebook skips finished runs. Delete `results/` after changing the configuration.
